the Somme was completed before the  president United States pulled the the  country out of the Paris climate climate  agreements you had some conversations  with him before that happened what gives  you some optimism that the president and  his cabinet which is filled with climate  change deniers can somehow do something  positive to turn the tide I tried hard  to convince Donald Trump before the  inauguration and after he got to the  White House to come to his senses on the  Paris agreement and I actually thought  there was reason to hope after my  exchanges with him that he would but I  but I was wrong  the rogues gallery of climate deniers  around him I think stopped that so we're  just going to have to work around him  and the good news is that there's a law  of physics that sometimes works in  politics for every action there is an  equal and opposite reaction and out in  the country right now there is a huge  reaction to what he's doing  and on the climate movement has more  momentum and enthusiasm and passion now  than I've ever seen and what Bonnie and  John have done with this movie my  dearest hope is that people will see it  and the more people who do the more the  movement will gain momentum so I'm very  optimistic I think we're going to win  this and after he pulled out of Paris I  was fearful that other countries might  use that as an excuse to do the same but  instead the entire rest of the world  redoubled its commitment to the Paris  agreement as if to say will show you  Donald wrong and and here here in the US  governor's and mayors and business  leaders have stepped up to fill the gap  and said we're still in and by the way  your Governor Jerry Brown is a real hero  on this and  now it looks like the US has a real  chance of meeting the commitments in  spite of Donald Trump Jeff I want to ask  about participants view in this position  in all this because you obviously worked  on the original film why did you feel it  was time 11 years later to come back and  revisit what Davis Guggenheim had  started with an inconvenient truth  well first off a big thank you to all of  you here tonight  coming up for this film the original  film was built on the shoulders of all  of those in the Academy and all of those  who did an inconvenient truth in the  first place like Davis Guggenheim who  directed the film and all through the  years  Davis along with our producers Laurie  David and Lawrence bender and Scott  Burns and and others unless they choked  up we would talk it is now a time to  come back and tell the world what has  happened because before the first film  came out Americans didn't care so much  about climate change and a couple of  years after it came out Americans cared  a lot the recession hit the deniers  gotten here and was no is that really  the time to come back and say what  happened well we tried in different ways  finally in April of 2015  al had come to my office a participant  and we were sitting together and saying  al is now the time for sequel and I'll  said you know it's been 10 years well  very wise they said you know this is a  10 year anniversary and this movie this  film had such a touch point in the world  and I'd like to get that message out to  the people that we talked to that what  the scientist said what the people said  what those results were and whether we  were right whether we were not right  what happened and also is there a new  story to tell and I said I agree  completely and there's this whole new  world of clean tech that you saw in the  film I work in my day job much of the  time in the clean tech and so I see that  these things are coming we're on the  precipice of a new generation of clean  energy for everyone so Al and I said you  know what now is the time to make the  film and I just have one promise what  one won't ask really let's not  make a political film it's about what  happened to climate and the clean tech  revolution remember we saw most of this  prior to the u.s. election so all I  could say is politics happens but the  clean tech revolution is upon us and Al  has been leading us all the way through  John and Bonnie how did you approach  this as a film that would both extend  the conversation that was started 11  years ago with the first film and make a  new conversation happen as well what was  the continuation and what was the new  thought that you brought to the making  of your film let's start well of course  we were you know so honored to get this  call from Diane wireman that have Jeff's  documentary department at participant we  we went to Nashville in July a couple  years ago almost exactly two years ago  we sat down without he was gracious  enough to give us the ten hour version  of his slide show I'll be showing that  later and Bonnie and I we had we had  worked on a climate film called the  island president but thank you about  four or five years ago and so we felt  like we knew kind of the basics but we  really learned a lot that day and one  thing we learned a lot as you just saw  about the about just how bad things have  gotten and of course how much hope there  is and the sustainability markets now  but we also just felt Al's energy and we  kind of looked at each other and we  thought if we could do a Verret a film a  cinema verite film where we were kind of  flies on the wall that sort of harkens  back to you know the way documentaries  were done back in the day which is of  course a format that we love to work and  we thought if we could capture that and  we looked at Al scheduled for the year  we thought oh god the Paris climate  Accords this is happening later this  year about that that seems pretty  interesting and we started thinking in  those terms and towards of sort of  building a documentary screenplay over  the course of the next 18 months Bonnie  how would you say that you cast the  planet in this film and how important  was it to get citizen journalists to be  involved much the way that their  reporting of what's happening in Syria  to document what's happening around  around the world  so planet Earth was really obviously the  major character  in this film and it was incredibly  important to us to make sure that the  cinematography reflected that that  whenever you see something of Earth it  is in its all its majestic beauty to  start out with but often like you see in  Greenland you're sort of you know you're  experiencing one of the wonders of the  world but then all of a sudden as we  pull up and John it's also the  cinematographer of this film and we had  some drones in Greenland that kind of  lifted up and you started to see the  cracks in the ice and kind of the virus  that that is taking over this wonder of  the world so we we looked for ways to  cinematically to show that dissonance  that was very important to us  and another key character in the film  was all the user-generated video from  citizens around the world who are  experiencing climate disasters a very  good example of this would be when we  did the research for the terrible storm  that hit Tacloban in the Philippines and  had a picture that and Richard was  responsible for a huge amount of this  research and collection but we wanted to  see what kind of user-generated videos  we could find on the Internet of people  who had been in the middle of the storm  trying to save people in fact the mayor  of Tacloban that video that you see  where he's pushing through the ceiling  to find dry ground that was his he was  taking it in the moment so people around  the world are feeling this it's  emotional for them and they're the small  the small acts of citizen journalism are  actually collectively having one voice  that we can all respond to Richard you  accompany these filmmakers to Paris and  part of the nature of the story is we're  going to follow the vice-president  around and the Vice President is meeting  with some very interesting and  high-profile people so you've got your  camera crew and then there's maybe a  head of state that wanders into the  frame what are the conversations that  you're having in real time to make sure  that your cameras are getting where the  Vice President is getting and you're  getting access to those conversations I  guess part of my job was to go into the  men's room and to see you with the  leaders were coming it going to be  coming out of it but  but now we were in in in Paris there's a  kind of inner sanctum that we had a path  to get into and it was amazing to see  you know  bonkie moon walk by and president  Hollande walked by and in obviously the  former vice president is completely  comfortable with these people and they  love to see him and it wasn't it wasn't  hard to necessarily arrange these  meetings I mean you see in the film  Justin Trudeau and Trudeau comes up and  and introduces himself as as if mr. Gore  doesn't know who he is and and it was  like that time and time again one of the  one of the moments that I thought was  particularly funny I didn't have to do  it that's where leaders was he would be  mobbed constantly and I remember you  saying these are my peeps  it's my demo and there were everywhere  we went people want to take selfies and  and it made you realize that the United  States is so unique in terms of this  polarization over the climate that  around the world there seems to be much  more consensus that we need to do  something about it and but we were we  were making our way to a hallway and  it's got to be so many people that  former vice president jumped into the  swedish off at the side days and they  were surprised to see you what is he  doing in there and quickly the kind of  the crowd dissipated and we've reached  into the into the hallway what can  people do leaving this theater what can  they do give them five things that's  going to help turn the tide use your  voice use your vote use your choices in  life your voice is important to win the  conversations about climate these other  revolutions that were referred to in the  movie were won in conversations before  the actual laws changed if somebody had  told me even five years ago that in the  year 2017 gay marriage would be legal in  all 50 states and accepted honored  celebrated by more than two-thirds of  the American people I would have said I  sure hope so but that seems impossible  but it happened because people created a  social revolution in consciousness and  awareness so use your voice including on  social media and using your vote also  means communicating with your elected  officials and the candidates who  approach you when enough people say  passionately this is important to you it  changes use your choices in life you've  made such choices a new electric car  just came out for $35,000 yesterday  there are companies in California who  will put solar panels all over your roof  for free and your bills will go down 20%  the next day the choices are becoming  available now and when you make the  right ones that sends a powerful message  to business and industry and for those  of you who like this movie please use  word of mouth to recommend it to others  because the more people who see this the  more this movement will gain momentum  [Applause]

